{"items":[{"targetId":"split-000003","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"Confluence compares reduction paths that start from the same net.","densityScore":0.81,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Defines the common starting point for competing choices."}]},{"targetId":"split-000004","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"A diamond closes when reducing the residual of each original step reaches an identical net.","densityScore":0.84,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Makes the required join between the two branches explicit."}]},{"targetId":"split-000005","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"Because an agent has only one principal port, it can participate in at most one active pair at a time.","densityScore":0.84,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains exclusive ownership of agents by active pairs."}]},{"targetId":"split-000006","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"Two active pairs sharing an agent would require that agent's single principal port to be connected twice.","densityScore":0.83,"relation":"supports","reason":"Derives disjointness from port uniqueness."}]},{"targetId":"split-000007","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Disjoint rewrites modify separate local subnets, so applying either one leaves the other's redex intact.","densityScore":0.85,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains why the two reductions commute."}]},{"targetId":"split-000008","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"It is a local property: it only compares two one-step reductions from one net.","densityScore":0.78,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Distinguishes the local diamond condition from global reduction behavior."}]},{"targetId":"split-000009","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"A system can have commuting local choices while still admitting an infinite reduction sequence.","densityScore":0.79,"relation":"supports","reason":"Separates confluence from termination."}]},{"targetId":"split-000010","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"For normalizing nets, interaction scheduling changes neither the normal form nor the number of core interactions needed to reach it.","densityScore":0.86,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"States the operational consequence of uniform normalization length."}]},{"targetId":"split-000011","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"Indexed fans distinguished sharing scopes by carrying administrative scope information in the graph.","densityScore":0.76,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains the purpose of the earlier representation."}]},{"targetId":"split-000012","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"These delimiter agents marked boundaries between nested sharing scopes.","densityScore":0.78,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains why delimiter constructs were introduced."}]},{"targetId":"split-000013","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"Administrative scope management can dominate the interaction count even when it performs no direct duplication work.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Makes the cost concern concrete."}]},{"targetId":"split-000014","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"Unbounded indices enlarge the representation carried by a net and complicate a fixed finite-agent implementation.","densityScore":0.81,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains why unbounded scope labels are undesirable."}]},{"targetId":"split-000015","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"The fixed auxiliary positions give each fan a stable semantic ordering independent of its drawn orientation.","densityScore":0.79,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Connects fan geometry to port identity."}]},{"targetId":"split-000016","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"The number of bottom ports records how many outgoing uses the replicator serves.","densityScore":0.79,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Relates the shape's arity to sharing."}]},{"targetId":"split-000017","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"A rectangle is graphical boundary notation, so it does not participate in interaction rules as an agent.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Separates diagram notation from computational entities."}]},{"targetId":"split-000018","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"When an eraser meets another agent, it propagates by creating erasers on that agent's auxiliary connections.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"enables","reason":"Explains how a principal-only eraser removes a subnet."}]},{"targetId":"split-000019","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"Arity zero represents no retained uses, while larger arities represent correspondingly many shared uses.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Clarifies the endpoints of natural-number arity."}]},{"targetId":"split-000020","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Its principal connection receives the shared value, and its auxiliary connections distribute that value to its uses.","densityScore":0.84,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains the direction of sharing through a replicator."}]},{"targetId":"split-000021","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"The level identifies a surrounding sharing stratum, while each delta records a local offset for one outgoing use.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Separates the roles of levels and deltas."}]},{"targetId":"split-000022","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"A linear binder therefore needs neither erasure nor duplication for its bound variable.","densityScore":0.81,"relation":"supports","reason":"Connects exact single use to the graph encoding."}]},{"targetId":"split-000023","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"An affine binder may require erasure when its bound variable has no occurrence.","densityScore":0.79,"relation":"supports","reason":"Connects optional use to erasure."}]},{"targetId":"split-000024","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"A relevant binder may require duplication when its bound variable has multiple occurrences.","densityScore":0.79,"relation":"supports","reason":"Connects mandatory use to possible sharing."}]},{"targetId":"split-000025","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"Full terms combine the possibilities of zero, one, or many variable occurrences.","densityScore":0.77,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Positions full terms relative to the restricted usage classes."}]},{"targetId":"split-000026","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"Together these agents encode the structural rules needed to represent unrestricted variable usage.","densityScore":0.84,"relation":"supports","reason":"Unifies the three structural roles."}]},{"targetId":"split-000027","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"Matching only the level is insufficient because different arities or delta assignments represent different sharing structures.","densityScore":0.83,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains every component of equality."}]},{"targetId":"split-000028","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"The translation invariant constrains active replicators so that a same-level encounter has matching arity and deltas.","densityScore":0.83,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains why level comparison works for translated terms."}]},{"targetId":"split-000029","injections":[{"type":"uncertainty","text":"Without the translation invariant, equal levels alone do not establish that the replicators have equal arity or deltas.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"supports","reason":"States the missing evidence in arbitrary nets."}]},{"targetId":"split-000030","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"The root induces a direction on each connection, letting the same local shape be classified by its position in the net.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains how orientation labels are determined."}]},{"targetId":"split-000031","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"This initial orientation makes the principal port the downstream exit of a fan-in and its auxiliaries upstream inputs.","densityScore":0.81,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Clarifies the fan-in orientation."}]},{"targetId":"split-000032","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Copying and rewiring during reduction can reverse the parent-child direction of a replicator's ports.","densityScore":0.78,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains why fan-outs arise after reduction."}]},{"targetId":"split-000033","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"A fan-out arises as the propagated counterpart of prior sharing, whereas an unused fan-in can remain unmatched.","densityScore":0.79,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains the asymmetric pairing condition."}]},{"targetId":"split-000034","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"A candidate pair must satisfy compatible level and port-delta constraints before it can be treated as matching sharing structure.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"enables","reason":"States how the annotations guide pairing."}]},{"targetId":"split-000035","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Annihilation removes both agents and directly reconnects their corresponding remaining ports.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Describes the effect of equal-agent interaction."}]},{"targetId":"split-000036","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"The translation invariant supplies the equality conditions needed for same-level active replicators to annihilate safely.","densityScore":0.84,"relation":"supports","reason":"Connects translated-term well-formedness to the rewrite rule."}]},{"targetId":"split-000037","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"Reconnecting the abstraction body and argument wiring performs substitution without textual copying.","densityScore":0.84,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains the lambda-calculus meaning of annihilation."}]},{"targetId":"split-000038","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"The interaction deletes the encountered agent's principal connection and propagates erasure through one selected auxiliary branch.","densityScore":0.78,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Clarifies the local erasure step."}]},{"targetId":"split-000039","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Each auxiliary output becomes the root of an erased subgraph, so both branches are discarded independently.","densityScore":0.81,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains why two erasers result."}]},{"targetId":"split-000040","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Erasure distributes over every outgoing sharing branch, producing one eraser per auxiliary port.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Generalizes the propagation rule to arbitrary arity."}]},{"targetId":"split-000041","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"Shape alone therefore does not determine whether a triangular fan represents application or abstraction.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"supports","reason":"Highlights the need for orientation-based interpretation."}]},{"targetId":"split-000042","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"A fan's direction toward or away from the root distinguishes how its principal and auxiliary connections are interpreted.","densityScore":0.81,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains role recovery from orientation."}]},{"targetId":"split-000043","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"The reconnection pairs auxiliary ports by their semantic indices rather than by incidental drawing proximity.","densityScore":0.83,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Clarifies the rewiring rule."}]},{"targetId":"split-000044","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"This convention fixes a port numbering for diagrams and determines which auxiliary connections are corresponding.","densityScore":0.78,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains why the orientation convention matters."}]},{"targetId":"split-000045","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"The rewrite connects the argument net to the abstraction's bound-variable interface and exposes the abstraction body.","densityScore":0.83,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Makes the beta-reduction correspondence concrete."}]},{"targetId":"split-000046","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"For an application, the first auxiliary connection conventionally carries the function and the second carries the argument.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Gives the semantic meaning of the port order."}]},{"targetId":"split-000047","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"The reversed visual order preserves the same semantic port indices after the abstraction fan is rotated.","densityScore":0.81,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains the reversed ordering convention."}]},{"targetId":"split-000048","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"Diagram geometry is secondary to port identity: a visual crossing can still represent the intended index-to-index reconnection.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Prevents interpreting crossing wires as changed semantics."}]},{"targetId":"split-000049","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Each copy retains the original fan's arity and auxiliary-port arrangement while serving one replicated branch.","densityScore":0.83,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Clarifies what an exact fan copy preserves."}]},{"targetId":"split-000050","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"The number of produced fans equals the replicator arity because each auxiliary port represents one required use of the copied structure.","densityScore":0.83,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains the copy count."}]}]}
